NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"faq","children":[{"type":"text","value":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"is-this-a-shiki-replacement","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Is this a Shiki replacement?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only for a narrower use case. TanStack Highlight is intended for blogs and docs with known languages. Shiki remains the better choice for TextMate accuracy, VS Code compatibility, or broad language coverage."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-not-use-sugar-high","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why not use Sugar High?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sugar High is an excellent smaller option for JavaScript, TypeScript, and JSX. Use TanStack Highlight when you also need isolated non-JavaScript languages, class-based compact output, embedded markup regions, Markdown adapters, or character-range decorations."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"can-it-run-in-the-browser","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can it run in the browser?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes. The core is synchronous and has no Node.js runtime dependency. Use selective language imports to keep the client bundle small."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"should-i-highlight-on-the-server-or-client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Should I highlight on the server or client?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use SSR for the initial page so code is styled before hydration. Reuse the same highlighter client-side for navigations, live previews, or content loaded after hydration. See "},{"type":"link","href":"guides/ssr-and-client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SSR and Client Rendering"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"does-it-detect-the-language-automatically","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Does it detect the language automatically?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No. Documentation pipelines already know the fence language, and detection would increase size, work, and ambiguity."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-are-embedded-script-or-style-contents-uncolored","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why are embedded script or style contents uncolored?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The delegated language is not registered. HTML does not import JavaScript or CSS automatically. See "},{"type":"link","href":"guides/embedded-languages","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Embedded Languages"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"can-i-import-a-vs-code-theme","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can I import a VS Code theme?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No. Themes are small semantic color objects. Create one directly or use a shipped isolated theme."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"can-css-add-font-styles","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can CSS add font styles?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes. Token classes are stable, so bold, italic, opacity, backgrounds, and contrast adjustments belong in your stylesheet."}]},{"type":"code","value":".th-comment { font-style: italic; }\n.th-keyword { font-weight: 600; }","lang":"css"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"does-it-sanitize-arbitrary-html","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Does it sanitize arbitrary HTML?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It escapes code text and decoration values. It is not a general HTML sanitizer. Only inject HTML returned directly by the highlighter, and sanitize untrusted surrounding Markdown with your normal pipeline."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-is-the-api-synchronous","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why is the API synchronous?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is no grammar loading or WebAssembly initialization. Synchronous behavior keeps SSR, hydration, and Markdown transforms straightforward."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"can-i-add-a-language","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can I add a language?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes. Register a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LanguageDefinition"},{"type":"text","value":" with a tokenizer that returns ordered semantic ranges. See "},{"type":"link","href":"guides/custom-languages","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Languages"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"are-malformed-programs-guaranteed-to-highlight-safely","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Are malformed programs guaranteed to highlight safely?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source is escaped and preserved, but token quality targets valid documentation code. The project does not promise compiler recovery behavior or exhaustive malformed-input conformance."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"faq","text":"FAQ","level":1},{"id":"is-this-a-shiki-replacement","text":"Is this a Shiki replacement?","level":2},{"id":"why-not-use-sugar-high","text":"Why not use Sugar High?","level":2},{"id":"can-it-run-in-the-browser","text":"Can it run in the browser?","level":2},{"id":"should-i-highlight-on-the-server-or-client","text":"Should I highlight on the server or client?","level":2},{"id":"does-it-detect-the-language-automatically","text":"Does it detect the language automatically?","level":2},{"id":"why-are-embedded-script-or-style-contents-uncolored","text":"Why are embedded script or style contents uncolored?","level":2},{"id":"can-i-import-a-vs-code-theme","text":"Can I import a VS Code theme?","level":2},{"id":"can-css-add-font-styles","text":"Can CSS add font styles?","level":2},{"id":"does-it-sanitize-arbitrary-html","text":"Does it sanitize arbitrary HTML?","level":2},{"id":"why-is-the-api-synchronous","text":"Why is the API synchronous?","level":2},{"id":"can-i-add-a-language","text":"Can I add a language?","level":2},{"id":"are-malformed-programs-guaranteed-to-highlight-safely","text":"Are malformed programs guaranteed to highlight safely?","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# FAQ\n\n## Is this a Shiki replacement?\n\nOnly for a narrower use case. TanStack Highlight is intended for blogs and docs with known languages. Shiki remains the better choice for TextMate accuracy, VS Code compatibility, or broad language coverage.\n\n## Why not use Sugar High?\n\nSugar High is an excellent smaller option for JavaScript, TypeScript, and JSX. Use TanStack Highlight when you also need isolated non-JavaScript languages, class-based compact output, embedded markup regions, Markdown adapters, or character-range decorations.\n\n## Can it run in the browser?\n\nYes. The core is synchronous and has no Node.js runtime dependency. Use selective language imports to keep the client bundle small.\n\n## Should I highlight on the server or client?\n\nUse SSR for the initial page so code is styled before hydration. Reuse the same highlighter client-side for navigations, live previews, or content loaded after hydration. See [SSR and Client Rendering](guides/ssr-and-client).\n\n## Does it detect the language automatically?\n\nNo. Documentation pipelines already know the fence language, and detection would increase size, work, and ambiguity.\n\n## Why are embedded script or style contents uncolored?\n\nThe delegated language is not registered. HTML does not import JavaScript or CSS automatically. See [Embedded Languages](guides/embedded-languages).\n\n## Can I import a VS Code theme?\n\nNo. Themes are small semantic color objects. Create one directly or use a shipped isolated theme.\n\n## Can CSS add font styles?\n\nYes. Token classes are stable, so bold, italic, opacity, backgrounds, and contrast adjustments belong in your stylesheet.\n\n```css\n.th-comment { font-style: italic; }\n.th-keyword { font-weight: 600; }\n```\n\n## Does it sanitize arbitrary HTML?\n\nIt escapes code text and decoration values. It is not a general HTML sanitizer. Only inject HTML returned directly by the highlighter, and sanitize untrusted surrounding Markdown with your normal pipeline.\n\n## Why is the API synchronous?\n\nThere is no grammar loading or WebAssembly initialization. Synchronous behavior keeps SSR, hydration, and Markdown transforms straightforward.\n\n## Can I add a language?\n\nYes. Register a `LanguageDefinition` with a tokenizer that returns ordered semantic ranges. See [Custom Languages](guides/custom-languages).\n\n## Are malformed programs guaranteed to highlight safely?\n\nSource is escaped and preserved, but token quality targets valid documentation code. The project does not promise compiler recovery behavior or exhaustive malformed-input conformance.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: FAQ\n---\n\n# FAQ\n\n## Is this a Shiki replacement?\n\nOnly for a narrower use case. TanStack Highlight is intended for blogs and docs with known languages. Shiki remains the better choice for TextMate accuracy, VS Code compatibility, or broad language coverage.\n\n## Why not use Sugar High?\n\nSugar High is an excellent smaller option for JavaScript, TypeScript, and JSX. Use TanStack Highlight when you also need isolated non-JavaScript languages, class-based compact output, embedded markup regions, Markdown adapters, or character-range decorations.\n\n## Can it run in the browser?\n\nYes. The core is synchronous and has no Node.js runtime dependency. Use selective language imports to keep the client bundle small.\n\n## Should I highlight on the server or client?\n\nUse SSR for the initial page so code is styled before hydration. Reuse the same highlighter client-side for navigations, live previews, or content loaded after hydration. See [SSR and Client Rendering](guides/ssr-and-client).\n\n## Does it detect the language automatically?\n\nNo. Documentation pipelines already know the fence language, and detection would increase size, work, and ambiguity.\n\n## Why are embedded script or style contents uncolored?\n\nThe delegated language is not registered. HTML does not import JavaScript or CSS automatically. See [Embedded Languages](guides/embedded-languages).\n\n## Can I import a VS Code theme?\n\nNo. Themes are small semantic color objects. Create one directly or use a shipped isolated theme.\n\n## Can CSS add font styles?\n\nYes. Token classes are stable, so bold, italic, opacity, backgrounds, and contrast adjustments belong in your stylesheet.\n\n```css\n.th-comment { font-style: italic; }\n.th-keyword { font-weight: 600; }\n```\n\n## Does it sanitize arbitrary HTML?\n\nIt escapes code text and decoration values. It is not a general HTML sanitizer. Only inject HTML returned directly by the highlighter, and sanitize untrusted surrounding Markdown with your normal pipeline.\n\n## Why is the API synchronous?\n\nThere is no grammar loading or WebAssembly initialization. Synchronous behavior keeps SSR, hydration, and Markdown transforms straightforward.\n\n## Can I add a language?\n\nYes. Register a `LanguageDefinition` with a tokenizer that returns ordered semantic ranges. See [Custom Languages](guides/custom-languages).\n\n## Are malformed programs guaranteed to highlight safely?\n\nSource is escaped and preserved, but token quality targets valid documentation code. The project does not promise compiler recovery behavior or exhaustive malformed-input conformance.\n"}
